# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A fence opens a block only where a block can start"

# TiddlyWiki reads a fenced block as a BLOCK rule, so three backticks continuing a paragraph open
# nothing — the parser reads them as an inline code span. Both readers agree here, and the agreement
# is worth pinning: a region opening mid-paragraph would carry the rest of the file as fenced
# content, and the two readers then pair every later backtick run differently.

Prose line
```ts
#^^^ text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5

A paragraph standing after it.
#^^^^^^^^^ text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5

# At a block start the same three backticks open the block they always did.

```ts
#^^ text.html.tiddlywiki5 markup.fenced_code.block.tiddlywiki5 meta.codeblock.tiddlywiki5 keyword.control.raw.fence.tiddlywiki5 punctuation.definition.raw.markup.begin.tiddlywiki5
let a = 1
```

A paragraph after the closed fence.
#^^^^^^^^^ text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
